{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The dashboard row that won't stop overflowing</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Three cards in a row, <code>display: flex</code>, each meant to share the space
      equally. Works fine — until one card gets a long filename or a long email address
      in it. That one card refuses to shrink, pushes past its share of the row, and the
      whole layout overflows sideways. You didn't set a width anywhere. Nothing looks wrong
      in the CSS.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Flex items have an invisible default: <code>min-width: auto</code>. For an item
      containing unbreakable content (a long word, a filename, a URL with no spaces),
      "auto" resolves to that content's own intrinsic width — the item is not allowed to
      shrink smaller than its own content, flex-shrink notwithstanding.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is the CSS Flexbox spec's "automatic minimum size" rule for flex items along
      the main axis. <code>flex-shrink: 1</code> only controls how items shrink relative
      to each other once they've reached their minimum sizes — it does not override that
      minimum. <code>min-width: auto</code> is that minimum, and for content-heavy items
      it's rarely 0.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>flex-shrink has a floor it won't cross</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Think of <code>flex-shrink</code> as "how eager is this item to shrink," and
      <code>min-width: auto</code> as "how small will it ever actually go." Even a very
      eager shrinker (<code>flex-shrink: 1</code> or higher) stops at its content's own
      width if you never override that floor.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Setting <code>min-width: 0</code> on the flex item removes that floor entirely,
      letting <code>flex-shrink</code> actually do its job all the way down to zero if
      needed. Pair it with <code>overflow: hidden</code> and <code>text-overflow: ellipsis</code>
      on the content inside so it truncates gracefully instead of just clipping mid-character.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The same rule applies on the cross axis for <code>flex-direction: column</code>
      layouts — swap in <code>min-height: 0</code> there instead. Grid has an equivalent
      gotcha with <code>minmax(0, 1fr)</code> vs. plain <code>1fr</code> for exactly the
      same underlying reason: an implicit auto-minimum sized to content.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>Why this specifically shows up in dashboards</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Dashboards are exactly where this bites hardest: a row of stat cards, a table row
      with a long value in one column, a breadcrumb bar with a long page title — anywhere
      real, unpredictable user data (not lorem ipsum) ends up inside a flex row that
      looked perfectly fine with placeholder text during development.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      It's rare in early development because test data is short and clean. It shows up in
      production, on one specific user's account, with one specific long value — which
      makes it feel like a data bug rather than the layout bug it actually is.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is also why the bug survives code review so often: the CSS is correct by every
      visual check the reviewer runs with realistic-looking short test content. The
      failure mode only appears with content wider than the container, which reviewers
      rarely deliberately test for.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

