{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Where else the same rule applies</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Same fix, same reasoning, in a <code>flex-direction: column</code> layout: use
      <code>min-height: 0</code> instead of <code>min-width: 0</code>, for the exact same
      reason on the other axis.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      CSS Grid has its own version: a plain <code>1fr</code> track can still refuse to
      shrink below its content's intrinsic size for the same automatic-minimum-size reason.
      <code>minmax(0, 1fr)</code> is the grid equivalent of <code>min-width: 0</code> on a
      flex item.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Nested flex containers compound this: if a parent flex item has no explicit
      <code>min-width: 0</code>, none of ITS children's shrink behavior can fix an
      overflow — the parent's own auto-minimum sets the floor for the whole subtree
      regardless of what's declared further down. Audit from the outside in, not the
      inside out, when hunting this bug in a deeply nested layout.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

