{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">Flex items default to min-width: auto, which for content-heavy items means "never smaller than my own content." flex-shrink can't override that floor.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Set min-width: 0 on the flex item to remove the floor, then pair it with overflow: hidden + text-overflow: ellipsis on the inner content so it truncates instead of overflowing.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">This is the CSS Flexbox spec's automatic minimum size rule for flex items along the main axis — not a browser bug or inconsistency, identical across evergreen browsers.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">This mostly shows up in production, not development — test data is short and clean; real user data (long filenames, long emails) is what actually triggers it.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">It survives code review because the layout looks correct with realistic short test content — the failure only appears with content wider than the container.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Nested flex containers compound this: a parent item's own min-width: auto sets the floor for its whole subtree, regardless of what's declared on children further down. Audit outside-in.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">Column layouts need the same fix on the other axis: min-height: 0 instead of min-width: 0.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">CSS Grid's equivalent is minmax(0, 1fr) instead of a plain 1fr track — same automatic-minimum-size rule, different layout mode.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Audit deeply nested flex layouts from the outermost container inward — a missing min-width: 0 on a parent blocks every child's shrink behavior beneath it.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
